ittarz's postulate for the critical curve of the sq Ising antiferromagnet. A renormalization-group treatment of the hard-square lattice gas yields a critical activity , which is in agreement with series-expansion and finite-lattice estimates but at variance with the postulated . The same calculation gives for the correlation-length exponent, thus supporting the conjecture that the transition of the hard-square lattice gas belongs to the Ising universality class.\n\nPhys. Rev. B 21, 4012– Published 1 May, 1980\n\nVol. 21, Iss. 9 — 1 May 1980\n\n  * Received 11 January 1980\n\n[Button: Export Citation]\n\nReuse & Permissions\n\nDOI: https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.21.4012--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe near-critical planar FK-Ising model (https://www.emergentmind.com/papers/1111.0144)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.0 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Second, a phenomenon about the near-critical behavior of FK-Ising is highlighted, which is completely missing from the case of standard percolation: in any monotone coupling of FK configurations $\\omega_p$ (e.g., in the one introduced in [Gri95]), as one raises $p$ near $p_c$, the new edges arrive in a self-organized way, so that the correlation length is not governed anymore by the number of pivotal edges at criticality.\n\n# The near-critical planar FK-Ising model\n\nPublished 1 Nov 2011 in math.PR, math-ph, and math.MP | (1111.0144v4)\n\nAbstract: We study the near-critical FK-Ising model. First, a determination of the correlation length defined via crossing probabilities is provided. Second, a phenomenon about the near-critical behavior of FK-Ising is highlighted, which is completely missing from the case of standard percolation: in any monotone coupling of FK configurations $\\omega_p$ (e.g., in the one introduced in [Gri95]), as one raises $p$ near $p_c$, the new edges arrive in a self-organized way, so that the correlation length is not governed anymore by the number of pivotal edges at criticality.\n\nAbstract PDF Upgrade to Chat\n\nAuthors (3)\n\n  1. Hugo Duminil-Copin \n  2. Christophe Garban \n  3. Gábor Pete \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQuantum phase transition dynamics in the two-dimensional transverse-field Ising model - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9481121/)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.1 years ago; Crawled: last month; The near-critical region of continuous symmetry breaking phase transition is characterized by critical slowing down reflected in an asymptotically divergent relaxation time scale and correlation length. ... Furthermore, measurements in these systems yield spin configurations along a tunable axis, e.g., along σ^{z} or σ^{x}, which can give access to both the spin-spin correlation functions we considered (see Eq. 9) and the total Ising energy (see Eq.\nOur work provides a testing platform for higher-dimensional quantum simulators.\n\n* * *\n\nA joint effort of modern numerical methods reveals dynamical universality at a quantum phase transition in two dimensions.\n\n## INTRODUCTION\n\nThe near-critical region of continuous symmetry breaking phase transition is characterized by critical slowing down reflected in an asymptotically divergent relaxation time scale and correlation length. When a many-body system is quenched—driven across the critical point at a fixed rate—critical slowing down will prevent its order parameter from keeping up with what would have been the instantaneous equilibrium. In particular, the correlation length—hence, the size of the fluctuating domains within which the order parameter is approximately uniform—will lag behind the state implied by the externally imposed conditions. Thus, instead of an infinite range order predicted in equilibrium for the posttransition broken symmetry phase, one is left with a mosaic of fluctuating domains. -------------------------------